-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Why this licence and not MIT
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This is deliberately restrictive because the plan is to sell the plugin. Under a
permissive licence such as MIT, anyone could legally take the paid build, upload
it somewhere, and give it away - and you would have no standing to object. That
is not a hypothetical in the Minecraft plugin market.

If you would rather open-source it, MIT or Apache-2.0 are the usual choices, and
swapping this file is all it takes. A common middle path is to keep the core
proprietary and publish a free-tier build separately.

This is a plain-language starting point written by a programmer, not a lawyer,
and it is not legal advice. If real money starts flowing through this, get an
actual licence reviewed.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party components
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

TickTriage bundles no third-party code. It compiles against, but does not
redistribute:

  paper-api        (Paper / PaperMC)          - GPL-3.0 / MIT, compileOnly
  worldguard       (EngineHub)                - LGPL-3.0,     compileOnly
  griefprevention  (TechFortress)             - MIT,          compileOnly

These are provided by the server at runtime and are not shipped in the jar.
Confirm each project's current terms before you publish.
